{{also|toten}}
==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{m|goh|tōden}}, compare {{etyl|non|-}} {{m|non|deyða}} (> {{etyl|da|-}} {{m|da|døde}}, {{etyl|sv|-}} {{m|sv|döda}}), {{etyl|got|-}} {{m|got|𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽}} and {{etyl|nl|-}} {{m|nl|doden}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈtøːtən/|[ˈtøːtən]|[ˈtøːtn̩]|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-töten.ogg|audio|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-weak|tötet|tötete|getötet}}

# to [[kill]]
#: {{usex|Zehn Menschen wurden bei dem Anschlag '''getötet'''.|t=Ten people were '''killed''' in the attack.|lang=de}}

====Usage notes====
* In formal style, ''töten'' is an adequate translation of English “to kill” in its literal meaning. Colloquially, the verb {{m|de|umbringen}} is preferred and ''töten'' is rare. In particular, only ''umbringen'' is generally used in figurative senses. For example: ''Ich bring meinen Freund um, wenn er meine Blumen nicht gegossen hat!'' – “I'll kill my boyfriend if he hasn't watered my flowers!”

